00:08:27.131 --> 00:08:28.120
then we were like, screw it, let's do it.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.123
And now let's talk about it.

00:08:29.123 --> 00:08:32.030
It's called "Unattended Baggage".

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.337
We're like, let's put Cotton
Candy Randy, Damnyell,

00:08:36.216 --> 00:08:41.216
in an environment, let's
put 'em in an airport

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:44.350
where they work, we just thought
it would be funny to like,

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.570
even though you could go anywhere

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.883
in the world of cartoon animation,

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.510
we just thought it was ironic to like-

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:53.450
- Yeah, it was the built-in irony.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.550
- Pin them down in the place

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:58.440
that everybody wants to escape
from and that's an airport.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.830
There's lots of different
places you can work,

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.450
there's lots of like
workplace dynamics there.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:04.547
So we started to build out
what that might look like.

00:09:04.547 --> 00:09:06.870
- It was based on the one time

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.531
that I think Damnyell and
Cotton Candy Randy got together

00:09:09.531 --> 00:09:12.650
in either a GMM or a GM More episode

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:17.570
and we were like, you know,
these guys are so other worldly.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:19.360
I mean, literally in some ways,

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.740
that this seems like the perfect thing

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:25.500
to put them together in an
animated show and you know,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.010
but the idea was okay, so
you got me as Damnyell,

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:31.520
you got Jordan as Cotton Candy Randy,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.350
and then, well,
- I should be in it too.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.230
- You know, this needs to be a trio.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:39.980
And so the original
idea basically just had

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:43.500
you as kind of a grounded character.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.630
I don't know exactly, your name
may have already been Leroy

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:48.070
but you were essentially Link

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:49.670
and you were using your regular voice

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:54.435
and we actually did a whole, in fact,

00:09:54.435 --> 00:09:59.420
the animation and
everything was originally

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.160
just you talking in your normal voice.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.680
- Then we got, we got
to get a funnier voice.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.460
This character needs to be funny.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.900
- Too much opportunity for
this character to be funny

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:08.890
and so then you basically ended up

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:11.733
kind of doing your forest patrol voice.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.620
It's essentially the same guy.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.570
- 'Cause as much as people want
forest patrol to come back,

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:23.490
it costs a lot of money to
animate and we couldn't,

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:27.230
we just can't justify or
support that just online.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:28.320
- Again, you make a certain-

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.180
- I'll just take that characterization,

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.330
I'll bake it into this character
and we'll see what happens.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.733
- I thought it was a big improvement.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.660
I gotta say, I mean, are we,

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.640
listen, I don't want this to be a downer,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.580
but I am going to just be honest with you

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:48.349
as we revisit these
ideas and I still think

00:10:48.349 --> 00:10:50.910
that "Unattended Baggage"
could be a great show,

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.820
especially in the way that we
kind of developed the pitch

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.050
even after we developed the sizzle reel.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.480
But I got to say-
- And we should talk about,

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.840
I want to say what that
is because I think that's,

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:03.180
strength of the show is
actually not in the sizzle reel

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.620
because we got going, we wrote
something, it was animated,

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.600
and then we were like, you know what?

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:10.920
And it was called Fairview,

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.850
'cause Fairview International
was the name of the airport.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.410
But then we started to really
dig into the characters

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.650
and we were like, I think
there's something about

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:24.255
dealing with masculinity,
toxic masculinity,

00:11:24.255 --> 00:11:26.790
stereotypical masculinity,

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:30.830
and guys getting in
touch with their feelings

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:34.380
and their trauma, potentially.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:36.730
- Well, and every one of
the characters ended up,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.785
it was very clear every
one of the characters

00:11:39.785 --> 00:11:42.883
had some baggage, had
some psychological things,

00:11:42.883 --> 00:11:44.710
some trauma in their background

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:46.150
that really contributed to who they were

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:47.680
and actually contributed to who they were

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.030
and what made them funny.

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:54.030
- So let's play with that,
like within their world,

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:59.840
let's actually grapple with therapy

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.050
and what their need for therapy-

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:02.900
- Informally.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:06.470
- Informally through other characters.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:10.190
And then we changed the title
to "Unattended Baggage".

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.145
- And every episode was going to be,

00:12:13.145 --> 00:12:15.420
or not necessarily every episode,

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.510
but in many different
episodes it was gonna be often

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.200
a device that we were going to use

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.500
was going to be a
flashback to the childhoods

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.860
of each one of our main three characters

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:27.670
so that you kind of
explore the relationship

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.420
with them and their parents
and then how they're as adults

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:32.370
having never properly dealt

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.950
with all their unintended baggage as kids

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:39.950
are kind of working that out,
to hilarious ends, as adults.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:42.760
So that's what the show became,

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.880
it's not really captured in the reel.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.675
The sizzle reel was like the
section of the pilot and-

00:12:48.675 --> 00:12:50.714
- That's what we were
excited about though,

00:12:50.714 --> 00:12:52.890
was when we got into the exploring

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:55.240
the guys and therapy of it all.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.030
- Yeah, I have absolutely no idea.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.150
I'm sure we'll see in the comments

00:12:59.150 --> 00:13:04.150
how people will respond to
us showing that to them.

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:05.293
I think people will-

00:13:05.293 --> 00:13:07.330
- Well we did it in a way
that was a bit confusing

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.370
because it's like, is
this real, was this real?

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:11.010
You know, I thought that was fun.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.040
- Have they really committed
this much to this bit

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.300
for him to show this?

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:15.730
- Now you know.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:17.980
- I think the show would be much better

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.720
than the sizzle reel
indicates, but you know,

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.620
you be the judge of what
you think this'll be.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.746
- Listen, if those people weren't laid off

00:13:24.746 --> 00:13:26.500
we'd be making the show right now.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:27.640
- 'Cause I also think that they understood

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.853
what we were doing and how
it had kinda updated and,

00:13:32.908 --> 00:13:34.150
I have mixed feelings about that one

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:36.850
'cause I still feel like
there is a viable show there.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:41.060
I just don't feel like we
executed the pitch that great

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:44.010
and then of course, things
outside of our control

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:44.880
caused it to fall through.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.970
- Okay, so that was like one
of the more recent ideas.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:51.360
I want to go back to one of the first

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:52.870
ideas that we came up with.

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:57.310
Now, we're going back to
the "Online Nation" days

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.840
so we were coming out here,
we were hosting the show,

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:02.200
you know, basically beginning to host it

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.693
as it was already
beginning to be canceled.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:09.260
One of the producers on the show, Paul,

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:12.800
was basically he became
our defacto manager

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.820
because every time we came
out here we were like,

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.060
okay, we got ideas.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:20.160
- We gotta get something else going.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.140
- We got stuff we want to do.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:27.130
Paul Cockrell, you know,
he was basically the guy

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.000
who discovered Pauly shore.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.230
The reason why Pauly shore
was the VJ personality

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.433
that he was on MTV was because of Paul.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:39.480
- And he was instrumental

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.920
in basically establishing Los Angeles

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.170
as a home for MTV as well, from New York.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:47.810
'Cause it had basically
been a New York thing.

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.230
- Yeah, he was the first L.A. guy.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:55.130
And then the producer of "Online
Nation" was David Hurwitz

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.870
who was an executive
producer on "Fear Factor"

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.150
back in the day, you know, years earlier.

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:02.670
- In fact, who famously said to us

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:06.290
while we were hosting
"Online Nation", more energy!

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:07.950
- More energy, that's all he would say.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:08.783
- More energy!

00:15:08.783 --> 00:15:10.850
- But Paul was more hands-on.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:12.886
- We say that as an inside joke.

00:15:12.886 --> 00:15:13.719
- So Paul became our defacto manager

00:15:13.719 --> 00:15:15.040
so he was the one that we were talking to

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:17.460
about all these ideas,
scripted and non-scripted,

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.123
and he was like, well,

00:15:19.123 --> 00:15:20.423
it was like, yeah, we got a scripted idea,

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:23.320
we can write a pilot.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:26.050
And we went back home

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:31.050
and we wrote what eventually
became we called "Legitimate".

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:35.053
- So that was before the reality ideas or?

00:15:36.191 --> 00:15:39.250
- I think at the same time, because-

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.987
- Because I've got the
script on my computer

00:15:41.987 --> 00:15:43.640
and it says copyright 2012.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:45.145
- I think at the same time

00:15:45.145 --> 00:15:47.750
we were also developing
non-scripted ideas,

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.360
we had two of those.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:51.043
- But we shot-

00:15:51.043 --> 00:15:52.180
- But we shot sizzle reels for those.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.102
- Those were before,

00:15:54.102 --> 00:15:54.935
because we still lived in North Carolina

00:15:54.935 --> 00:15:57.500
'cause I remember being in Lillington,

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.110
going around Lillington
shooting some of that stuff.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:00.943
- That's where we were

00:16:00.943 --> 00:16:03.100
when we started writing "Legitimate" too,

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.130
so they were happening at the same time.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.260
- Okay, so I guess it
was just 2012 is when-

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.050
- We can talk about
the non-scripted stuff,

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.300
we had this idea for a reality show,

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:14.070
kind of like "Dirty Jobs",

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.140
but instead of Mike Rowe it's
the two of us going around

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:22.140
to like redneck, I mean,
what I call redneck hobbies.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.220
- Lawnmower racing.
- Redneck past times.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:29.040
Yeah, lawnmower racing, noodling,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.040
that's where you, like you dig
your hand down into a river

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.980
and you get a cat fish to eat your hand.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.500
Like we were going to go do these things

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.385
and we were like, hey, we
know enough of these people,

00:16:40.385 --> 00:16:43.870
we can put something on tape so to speak

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:45.690
that can sell this idea.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.980
'Cause it's like, hey,
we still can tap into

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:49.640
that good old boy nature, you know,

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.050
we still live here in Harnett County.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:56.140
And my dad knew all of these guys

00:16:56.140 --> 00:17:00.300
who participated in
competitive lawnmower racing.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:01.757
And so we went out there

00:17:01.757 --> 00:17:03.860
and we got permission
to film the whole thing

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.820
and then we started interviewing
guys and like, I mean,

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:09.210
there's some funny moments when we were-

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.320
- We participated in the lawnmower race.

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:12.810
- These are not normal lawnmowers,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:16.320
they're like drag racing
versions of lawnmowers.

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:20.590
And they pull a sled in a straight line,

00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:22.730
whoever can get the
furthest the fastest wins.

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:26.773
And yeah, we talked a guy into
letting us ride his lawnmower

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:29.790
in the competition

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:31.570
and we make complete
fools out of ourselves

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:33.630
and it was perfect, in our mind.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.820
- It was funny, I mean, again-

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:37.700
- We called it "Good Old Boys".

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:39.760
- "Good Old Boys", I can't
help but be the, you know,

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:42.540
the 10 year later producer and be like,

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.730
oh, the fatal flaw with this idea

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:48.286
was that we assumed that we had the right.

00:17:48.286 --> 00:17:49.886
In other words, like, okay,

00:17:49.886 --> 00:17:53.420
"Dirty Jobs" and Mike Rowe
of makes sense a little bit,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.990
like it started as he
was like this guy doing

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:58.800
this bit on the radio
and he's this older guy

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:01.020
that's like highlighting
these people in America.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.830
We were just like two
dumb asses who were like

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:06.110
a little bit country, a
little bit rock and roll

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:07.070
and we thought we could go

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:09.360
and just hang out with
rednecks and have fun.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.660
And while I do think it
would have been a funny show,

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.840
people at the time were looking
for like, what's your in?

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.310
Like why you guys for this thing?

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:18.380
And so it just kind of died on the vine.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.250
- Like maybe it should
be Larry the Cable Guy,

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:21.976
is kind of what you're saying.

00:18:21.976 --> 00:18:22.809
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:22.809 --> 00:18:25.260
- But I mean, for the
video version of this thing

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:28.109
we can show you a clip,
we'll dig up the archives.

00:18:28.109 --> 00:18:30.859
(engine revving)

00:18:36.791 --> 00:18:39.410
At the same time we
developed another pitch,

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:41.180
'cause we told Paul, well, you know,

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:42.750
we also have engineering degrees,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:45.343
you know, we're smart guys.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:50.280
We developed a pitch
called "This or That".

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:51.570
- Now this was a good idea.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.290
- We couldn't call it "Would You Rather"

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.330
because "Would You Rather" was-

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:56.467
- It's copyrighted.

00:18:56.467 --> 00:18:58.400
- Trademarked or whatever it is as a game.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:01.660
We looked into licensing it,
like Paul looked into that

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:03.480
but like they wouldn't have any part of it

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:05.420
so we just changed it to this or that.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:08.251
But it was a, as far as I can remember,

00:19:08.251 --> 00:19:11.080
we would pick two things.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:12.710
Well, it was a would you rather scenario.

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:13.543
- It was would you rather-

00:19:13.543 --> 00:19:15.070
- That could actually be carried out.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:18.080
- And we would both do
one side of the thing

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:18.960
and come to the decision

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:20.890
of which one was the
actual correct answer.

00:19:20.890 --> 00:19:22.720
- We would actually definitively know

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.110
because we've experienced
both sides of it.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:27.820
- We shot a reel for that too.
- Would you rather-

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:29.570
- There was a fire hose
involved in one scene.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.910
- I think it was, would you rather be

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:38.540
pelted with a full blast of a fire hose

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.190
for X number of minutes

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:46.190
or have to walk around town an
entire day completely soaked.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:54.170
And so then we cut to a shot of one of us

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:57.210
dousing ourselves completely
with water from head to toe,

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:00.270
like boots filling up with
water in the Walmart parking lot

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.983
and then walking into Walmart.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.290
So you get an idea of, okay,

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:08.870
camera's going to follow around one of us

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:12.070
who is like constantly soaking himself

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:14.420
and trying to conduct normal life,

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:17.080
maybe get them a mortgage, I don't know.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:20.410
So it's a bit bitty, kind of sketchy,

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:22.660
but then we also cut to the other person

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:27.420
who was standing in front
of a a fireman with a hose

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:29.710
and he started blasting us with water.

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:30.630
- It was Joey Smith.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:31.490
- Yeah, 'cause back in Harnett County

00:20:32.838 --> 00:20:33.923
you got these connects.

00:20:34.823 --> 00:20:37.200
- He was a serving with the Lillington

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:40.760
volunteer fire department and
had access to a fire hose.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:44.480
And did we actually get sprayed
with a fire hose that day?

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:46.480
Or did we just make it look
like it was about to happen?

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:48.082
I can't remember.

00:20:48.082 --> 00:20:49.053
- I'll have to look at the footage.

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:56.530
I'm pretty sure that we
were just far enough away-

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:58.860
- That it wasn't crazy.
- That it wasn't that crazy,

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:01.090
but I might just be making up in my mind.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.410
The footage will tell.

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.640
- That particular example that we used

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:08.410
for the pitch is not
great, but the show still-

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:10.402
- There were other examples too.

00:21:10.402 --> 00:21:12.190
I think we had two more sets of examples,

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:13.900
but that's a fun idea.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:16.140
I think we revisited it a few times.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.570
- Maybe as a web series.
- As a web series.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:20.920
I'm surprised we didn't
pull the trigger on it

00:21:21.835 --> 00:21:24.357
'cause it's almost something
that we could do now.

00:21:24.357 --> 00:21:26.520
- Well we ended up doing would
you rather as a GMM segment

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:29.123
and that's when I baby birded
things into your mouth,

00:21:29.123 --> 00:21:31.140
it was just made the smaller,

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.070
more producible version of that.

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:35.470
- The last thing I remember
we did with Paul was

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:38.760
instead of us coming out to L.A. one time,

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:41.650
he said that he was coming
out to Maryland for something.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:43.820
And he said, you boys need to drive up

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.320
because the Discovery
Channel headquarters.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.153
- Silver Springs, Maryland.

00:21:47.153 --> 00:21:48.140
- Is in Silver Springs, Maryland,

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:49.940
right on the edge of Washington D.C.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:55.810
That's where you had to
meet with development

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:58.440
if you wanted to have a show
on the Discovery Channel.

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:00.170
And you know, we really thought

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:02.110
because we had engineering degrees

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:04.580
we should be able to come up with an idea.

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:06.914
Did we take this or that to them?

00:22:06.914 --> 00:22:09.970
I think we took a couple of other ideas,

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:11.518
but I remember it being-

00:22:11.518 --> 00:22:12.351
- I can't remember all the ideas.

00:22:12.351 --> 00:22:13.980
- It was more of a meet
and greet meeting too

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.400
where it was like, yeah,
we should work together.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.790
Sometimes it's just, it's
not about the pitches,

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:22.310
It's just about getting to know, like-

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.580
- I remember the, I remember an idea

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:27.193
that we pitched to them
and I still, I'm afraid to,

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.310
I still think this is a
great idea for a show.

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:34.160
It was our answer to a
man on the street show,

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:35.963
It was our answer to "Cash Cab"

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:39.720
and it was called "Guesstimaters".

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.820
- Oh, well yeah, we brought
that back as "The Magic Number",

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:43.790
we'll talk about it later.

00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:48.010
- But do you remember, so
the "Guesstimaters" idea,

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:49.940
which yes, we brought
back as a different idea

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:50.780
that was more contained

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:53.810
but the original idea for "Guesstimaters"

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:57.520
was that we would be, and
this is sort of based on,

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.220
you know, the thing that
we do on GMM sometimes

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:02.630
which is we try to guess
a number of something.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:04.460
But this was like, you're on the street

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:06.740
and let's say one episode
takes place in New York City

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:10.060
and so we walk up to
somebody on the street

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.430
and we say, sir, right there,

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:14.330
you see the Empire State Building,

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:18.410
how many windows, individual windows

00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:21.730
do you think are on the
Empire State Building?

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:24.100
If you can get within 10

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:26.577
you're going to win $1,000 right
now or something like that.

00:23:26.577 --> 00:23:28.250
And so then it would be like,

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.660
they'd guess and he would
be wrong or he'd be right.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:31.810
But then there would be this

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:35.010
like graphical sort of like animated thing

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:36.980
where it would be like, here's
the empire state building

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:40.110
and all of a sudden like
animated like overlay on it

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:41.660
would show you like,
there's this many windows

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.250
and they're made out of
this and they're this big

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:45.324
and they attach in this
way and there's this many.

00:23:45.324 --> 00:23:48.093
- You'd learn all this
stuff through the animation.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:51.730
But you would also get if
you're one of those people

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:54.527
like you, that really
loves to guesstimate things

00:23:54.527 --> 00:23:56.410
and is actually really good at it.

