- Now to be clear, we're
talking about the daughter,

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:08.540
the super ambitious daughter

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:11.280
who's the real sort of business mind,

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.850
she's so focused on Beef Queen

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:16.070
that she doesn't have time

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:19.170
for someone who's going
to like challenge her

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:21.230
So she needs somebody who's going to do

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:23.068
exactly what she says.

00:35:23.068 --> 00:35:25.580
- Danielle fell in love with him

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:28.290
on an episode of "Undercover Boss".

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:29.850
She also became an unintentional meme

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:30.910
for complimenting Kevin

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:33.570
on how he handled his
meat while on the show.

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:35.390
Kevin is remarkably naive about everything

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:38.666
outside of the small town
where he grew up and worked

00:35:38.666 --> 00:35:39.860
until marrying Danielle.

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:42.904
He is continuously amazed
by life in the big city.

00:35:42.904 --> 00:35:45.500
- Like he hasn't seen an escalator before,

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:47.350
everything is new to him.

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:48.980
- He's incredibly ignorant of all things

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:52.220
that Maze hold sacred,
which infuriates Maze.

00:35:52.220 --> 00:35:53.890
Danielle thought it would be a good idea

00:35:53.890 --> 00:35:57.860
to have Kevin run Love
Queen along with Maze,

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.890
hoping that his Beef Queen
loyalty and apolitical nature

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:04.070
would help take some of the
edge off of Love Queen's

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:06.020
more questionable operators.

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:08.470
- Right, so you've got
this basically sort of

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:11.900
conservative guy that hasn't
experienced a lot of the world

00:36:11.900 --> 00:36:15.500
and then you've got this over
the top hippie left-winger

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:17.560
and they have to work together

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:21.334
kind of in the context
of this sub organization

00:36:21.334 --> 00:36:25.650
that's the bigger context of
this giant fast food chain.

00:36:25.650 --> 00:36:29.360
I still like this idea.
- This is a fun idea.

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:33.050
We pitched this idea to
Adam McKay's company.

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:34.700
- Oh yeah, we did.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:37.040
- You know, we go into
Adam McKay's office.

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:38.860
- He wasn't there.
- He wasn't there,

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:41.550
but his business partner
and development guy,

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:43.890
I'm sorry I can't remember
his name but, was there

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:45.657
and we had a good conversation.

00:36:45.657 --> 00:36:49.507
- Just to be clear, 'cause
I don't want people to be,

00:36:49.507 --> 00:36:52.000
- Oh gosh, yeah, we
need to have this aside.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:53.924
- I want to say this real quick, okay,

00:36:53.924 --> 00:36:55.700
sometimes we go out on a limb

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:58.670
and we talk about sort of
things like this, you know,

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:01.240
not private but things that
you didn't know about before.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:05.460
Like a prime example is
several years ago when

00:37:05.460 --> 00:37:09.220
I auditioned to be Taserface
for "Guardians of the Galaxy".

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:11.220
And I told the story of the audition

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:14.730
and all I said was that
the audition went well

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:17.750
and that there was like a
second level of interest.

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:19.530
It wasn't like they just, you know,

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:22.401
I heard through the grapevine that they,

00:37:22.401 --> 00:37:26.360
and the director, James
Gunn, liked the audition,

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:27.840
and that's all I said, right.

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:29.942
- And we thought it would
be cool to tell you that.

00:37:29.942 --> 00:37:33.780
- That got turned into
someone, one of you,

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:37.960
going on to James Gunn's Instagram page

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:40.830
and commenting on a
completely unrelated post

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.150
and asking him the question saying-

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:43.660
- He was answering questions on Twitter.

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:45.120
Now we don't know who made the post.

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:46.170
- I think it was on Instagram.

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:47.770
- If you're starting to
get embarrassed right now

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:50.329
we do not know who you are,
we didn't pay attention

00:37:50.329 --> 00:37:52.097
and we're not holding it against you.

00:37:52.097 --> 00:37:54.522
And we don't remember your
name so don't sweat it.

00:37:54.522 --> 00:37:57.460
- But what you said was, is it true

00:37:57.460 --> 00:38:01.370
that Rhett McGlaughlin was the
first choice for Taserface.

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:04.920
At which point James Gunn
literally responds to that

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:06.363
in the comments and says,

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:10.320
no, Chris Sullivan was
always our first choice

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:12.750
although I liked that guy, or whatever.

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:14.023
So he acknowledged.

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:19.510
- So it seems like you were
like saying publicly that like

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:21.360
you turned down the role.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:23.450
- So now James Gunn thinks that I think

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:26.030
that I was the first choice for Taserface.

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:27.540
That's not what I said

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:29.470
and I regret even and
saying anything about it

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.530
but now you're talking
about us going into-

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:32.960
- So don't tweet at Adam McKay

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:34.980
thinking you're doing
us any favors by saying,

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:37.160
you should really do
that "Beef Queen" idea,

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:39.000
he won't know what the
hell you're talking about

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:40.501
and it'll embarrass us.

00:38:40.501 --> 00:38:43.633
- Just keep it to yourself, okay?

00:38:45.308 --> 00:38:46.270
- And if you want to
know what it feels like

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:49.050
to be our children, this is
how we talk to them sometimes.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:50.983
Don't embarrass me in public, okay?

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:54.220
And if you want to know

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:57.260
how my kids have to treat me
sometimes, they also tell me,

00:38:57.260 --> 00:38:59.910
hey, don't embarrass
me in public sometimes.

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:01.010
- I still like that idea.

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:03.300
- I still like, yeah, it was a fun idea.

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:06.300
I mean, we, you know, you go
around, you meet with people

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:08.293
who you think can produce this thing.

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:11.050
I'm not going to mention the other people

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:12.260
'cause now I'm regretting it.

00:39:12.260 --> 00:39:15.660
- And also we didn't, we
just did a write-up for this,

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.240
we didn't write the pilot.

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:19.310
And also, and this is something, you know,

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:21.260
this is something that we continue to

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:25.683
debate amongst ourselves and that is,

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:31.640
are we just writing this and
making this or producing this?

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:34.020
Are we doing that and also starring in it?

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:37.500
Obviously with "Buddy
System" we did all that,

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:40.440
but I don't know, the
more time that passes

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:43.530
the more convinced we become
that like, you kind of have to,

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:45.460
I don't know, you kind
of have to make a choice.

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:47.610
So I don't know if you're
going to get into any ideas,

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.470
it's more recent that
we've developed ideas

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:51.230
that don't have us being the two,

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:52.810
it doesn't start with two guys.

00:39:52.810 --> 00:39:54.100
- Here's some you won't remember.

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:57.100
I'll just read the one, do you
remember a show called "SOS"?

00:39:58.250 --> 00:39:59.833
- That was just a log line.

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:03.860
Just begin to read it and
then I'll remember it.

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:05.550
- Two brothers in law

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:09.730
who happened to deeply hate
one another find themselves-

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:12.720
- Oh, um, stranded on an island.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:13.820
- Marooned on an island

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:15.760
after going overboard during a flight

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:17.880
on a family cruise vacation.

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:19.930
They need overcome their
differences to survive

00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:22.010
on this island full of secrets.

00:40:22.010 --> 00:40:25.610
Think "Lost" meets "Last Man on Earth".

00:40:25.610 --> 00:40:26.443
That was it.

00:40:26.443 --> 00:40:28.897
- Yeah and it was going to
be like a surreal comedy.

00:40:28.897 --> 00:40:30.600
- "Sunshine Ranch".

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:34.880
- Ooh, um, you gotta
give me the first bit.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:39.880
- People come to Sunshine Ranch, Sunsign,

00:40:40.320 --> 00:40:41.650
to deal with addiction,

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:46.480
but not the typical vices
of drugs, sex, and alcohol.

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:51.050
No, the staff of sunshine
ranch specialize in...

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:52.070
- Oh gosh, I can't remember this.

00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:53.790
- Strange addictions.

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:56.270
There's a woman who's attracted
to inflatable furniture.

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:57.610
There's even a dude who's addicted

00:40:57.610 --> 00:40:59.810
to staring at his legs in a mirror.

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:02.320
Kurt finds himself sent to Sunshine Ranch

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:04.250
after a family intervention
over his addiction

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:08.100
to new car smell, which has
left him deeply in debt.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:09.370
- This is a dumb idea.

00:41:09.370 --> 00:41:10.700
- He insists he's normal.

00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:12.980
Ensemble cast, Rhett
and Link are patients,

00:41:12.980 --> 00:41:14.880
or potentially patient slash staff.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:16.280
- That's a dumb idea.

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:17.720
- Potentially offensive.

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:19.260
- Yeah, it's also like insensitive.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:20.510
- Wrong minded.
- Yeah.

00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:21.370
Yup, not proud of that one.

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:22.880
- Making fun of people's addictions.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:25.010
I didn't read it to myself
before I read it to everyone.

00:41:25.010 --> 00:41:27.927
- No, it's fine, it's
good, we go into places

00:41:27.927 --> 00:41:30.247
and you gotta just let
the ideas flow sometimes.

00:41:30.247 --> 00:41:31.637
- "Opinionated".

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.763
This one starts off, Rhett and Link bond.

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:39.650
I don't even know if this is scripted

00:41:40.930 --> 00:41:43.240
Rhett and Link bond over pointing out

00:41:43.240 --> 00:41:45.140
what's annoying about other people.

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:46.310
I think that's just a statement,

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:48.400
that's a backstory statement.

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:50.310
This gives them the
idea to start a business

00:41:50.310 --> 00:41:53.400
which offers a very simple
yet specialized service.

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:54.890
- I remember this.

00:41:54.890 --> 00:41:56.590
- Spend one hour with someone

00:41:56.590 --> 00:42:00.010
and then tell them everything
about them that's annoying.

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:02.210
Can their friendship and
business partnership withstand

00:42:02.210 --> 00:42:04.400
one another's razor-sharp criticism?

00:42:04.400 --> 00:42:06.380
- So the premise for this,

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:08.450
and again, this is not
a developed show idea,

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:12.277
but it is based on the
idea that sometimes,

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:17.140
and this may look make
us look like assholes

00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:19.250
and we're not assholes to people directly,

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:21.237
Let me just be honest about
that or just get that out there.

00:42:21.237 --> 00:42:26.070
But you know, especially
back before the pandemic

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:28.210
when we actually would
go to social functions

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:29.930
and meet new people,

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:31.430
you know, sometimes
you're like go to a party

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:32.800
and you'll meet somebody

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:35.490
and then we'll begin
to have a conversation

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:37.720
after the party and be
like, did you meet that guy?

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:39.100
Did you meet Kurt?
- Right.

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:42.040
- And it's like, oh yeah.

00:42:42.040 --> 00:42:45.730
And did you notice the thing
that he did with his so and so?

00:42:45.730 --> 00:42:48.400
- It's more observational
humor, but yeah, it's like,

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:51.310
we like picking people apart
and we also knew that like,

00:42:51.310 --> 00:42:53.390
if you took people who
did that to an extreme,

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:54.750
they would be assholes

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:56.530
and they probably couldn't
live with each other.

00:42:56.530 --> 00:42:59.480
- And let me be clear, so
Kurt Sutter is a friend,

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:01.510
I'm not talking about Kurt Sutter.

00:43:01.510 --> 00:43:03.373
I just used the name Kurt.

00:43:04.399 --> 00:43:06.190
- I think Kurt was in the last pitch.

00:43:06.190 --> 00:43:08.990
- There's nothing annoying about Kurt.

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:11.573
- All right, let me go to feature ideas.

00:43:15.449 --> 00:43:16.282
Okay.

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:18.980
The beast is real.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:21.064
I definitely remember this one.

00:43:21.064 --> 00:43:22.647
This one says 2018.

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:25.290
Two friends are deep in the wilderness,

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:27.990
dead set on locating
a fabled forest beast,

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:30.160
think Bigfoot but cooler.

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:33.640
But it turns out that they're escapees

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:36.530
from a mental hospital,
what, we can't do that.

00:43:36.530 --> 00:43:38.493
- But hold on. just to be clear, like,

00:43:39.353 --> 00:43:42.430
this is one of those, and I do question

00:43:42.430 --> 00:43:43.630
whether or not you can do this anymore

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:44.980
and I realized it was only 2018

00:43:44.980 --> 00:43:46.330
that we came up with this idea.

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:51.330
But the idea was that you've
got someone that is wrongfully,

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:56.030
has been wrongfully committed.

00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:58.629
- Yeah, I mean, we both been
watching "The Leftovers"

00:43:58.629 --> 00:44:00.210
and there's a lot of that going on there.

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:03.710
There's a guy in a, the
dad's in a mental institute.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:05.960
- It's sort of blurring the idea between,

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:07.750
you know, reality and
fantasy and you're like,

00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:12.413
is this person like
certifiable or do, you know?

00:44:12.413 --> 00:44:14.990
- It raises the question,
the question from-

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:18.380
- If you say that you've had
an experience with a Sasquatch,

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:20.050
you know, most people think

00:44:20.050 --> 00:44:21.910
there's something not
quite right with you.

00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:25.730
But these guys have this
commitment to finding this thing

00:44:25.730 --> 00:44:26.563
and there's a-

00:44:26.563 --> 00:44:28.120
- Their resident psychiatrist Julie,

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:29.470
who has played along with the guys'

00:44:29.470 --> 00:44:30.970
imaginative ideas for years,

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:33.010
joins their journey in an
effort to bring them home

00:44:33.010 --> 00:44:34.670
safely and unharmed.

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:37.720
When Julia is accidentally
shot by one of them

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.700
they must abandon their journey
and bring her to safety.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:41.680
Things are complicated

00:44:41.680 --> 00:44:44.100
when they actually
encounter the beast itself,

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:46.330
boom, spoiler alert!

00:44:46.330 --> 00:44:49.410
Tone is comedic, but gripping and real.

00:44:49.410 --> 00:44:51.296
Kind of like "Swiss Army Man"

00:44:51.296 --> 00:44:53.504
meets "O Brother, Where Art
Thou?" meets "Deliverance".

00:44:53.504 --> 00:44:54.903
(Rhett and Link laughing)

00:44:54.903 --> 00:44:57.780
- Again, this was not-
- It's just an idea.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:58.910
Just throw ideas against the board.

00:44:58.910 --> 00:44:59.850
- It hasn't been developed.

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.410
- We didn't pitch that to anybody.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:04.317
- No, I don't think we,
yeah, it's got some flaws.

00:45:04.317 --> 00:45:07.440
- "Charlie Horse Saves the World".

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:11.940
This was 2018 but this is
something that was percolating

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:14.300
for many years.

00:45:14.300 --> 00:45:16.850
The way I remember this
idea starting, and I'm not,

00:45:16.850 --> 00:45:19.030
I'll let you share the idea
as much as you remember it

00:45:19.030 --> 00:45:19.880
or I can read it,

00:45:22.128 --> 00:45:25.560
we had these meetings
with production companies

00:45:25.560 --> 00:45:29.640
and I think Paul set this up
way back then where we would go

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:34.010
and we would just have meet
and greet meetings and say,

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:35.750
yeah, we've got a bunch of ideas

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:38.350
but this is more about just
knowing that we're here now.

00:45:38.350 --> 00:45:40.160
Look at us Rhett and Link, we're in L.A.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:42.410
- General meetings.
- Generals they call it.

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:45.950
You go in, you sit in the lobby,

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:48.810
and somebody at it behind
a desk would like to say,

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:50.880
would you like some coffee
or tea while you wait

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:55.060
up to 40 minutes to go back
for this general meeting

00:45:55.060 --> 00:45:56.823
that is no one's priority.

00:45:57.810 --> 00:45:59.540
And we'd say, sure,
the best thing about it

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:00.990
was getting the drinks and sitting there

00:46:00.990 --> 00:46:02.950
and looking at the posters on the wall

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:05.080
of all the stuff they've made.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:08.930
- And we didn't like, we still
don't like general meetings

00:46:08.930 --> 00:46:11.470
because we're like, why
are we talking about

00:46:11.470 --> 00:46:14.400
things in general when we can
talk about a specific idea?

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:16.650
And so we just kind of lost our appetite

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:17.483
'cause we were like,

00:46:17.483 --> 00:46:19.104
if we were having a general
meeting with somebody,

00:46:19.104 --> 00:46:21.730
it's like, well okay, what
are we going to do together?

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:24.480
- We had a few people would
say, do you have any ideas?

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:27.330
And we'd be like, yeah, we're
working on a bunch of stuff.

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:28.260
We can come back to you.

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:30.120
And it felt like we were
like keeping things secret

00:46:30.120 --> 00:46:31.800
but we really didn't have any ideas.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.810
- So what we ended up doing that day

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:35.990
is we were looking at
the posters in the lobby,

00:46:35.990 --> 00:46:38.010
