00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:52.210
featured on the broadcast.

00:57:52.210 --> 00:57:54.060
Of course they're much older now.

00:57:54.060 --> 00:57:55.010
The American celebrities,

00:57:55.010 --> 00:57:56.640
more than a decade past their prime,

00:57:56.640 --> 00:57:58.890
are essentially prisoners on Glondor

00:57:58.890 --> 00:58:00.990
living in a small Earth-like village.

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:03.190
the Glondorian's did the best they could

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:05.430
but they messed up a bunch of details.

00:58:05.430 --> 00:58:08.830
And they're forced to hang out
with Dale whenever he wants.

00:58:08.830 --> 00:58:12.020
They also make appearances at
important Glondorian events.

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:13.640
They're basically like the royal family.

00:58:13.640 --> 00:58:14.920
- The royal family, but they're old

00:58:14.920 --> 00:58:16.690
and they can't do any of the
stuff that they used to do.

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:19.080
Like, you know-
- Washed up.

00:58:19.080 --> 00:58:20.800
- Washed up.
- Pop culture icons.

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:23.840
- D celebrities.

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:26.660
- The next one in the writeup
is "Fairview International"

00:58:26.660 --> 00:58:29.630
which became "Unattended Baggage",

00:58:29.630 --> 00:58:32.400
and so in this particular document,

00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:35.740
that was the second
thing that we put on here

00:58:35.740 --> 00:58:37.170
with much less fanfare

00:58:37.170 --> 00:58:39.690
is the thing that we ended up developing.

00:58:39.690 --> 00:58:42.782
We also had Norman Normal on here.

00:58:42.782 --> 00:58:44.757
That was a really old
animated idea we had.

00:58:44.757 --> 00:58:47.370
- Norman Normal actually made,

00:58:47.370 --> 00:58:50.270
actually went really far down
the rabbit hole with that

00:58:50.270 --> 00:58:51.400
because we were working.

00:58:51.400 --> 00:58:55.160
- Some producers out of New Zealand

00:58:55.160 --> 00:58:59.980
or Australia contacted us in
our basement in Lillington.

00:58:59.980 --> 00:59:01.390
- This is long time ago.

00:59:01.390 --> 00:59:03.600
- This was like before "Online Nation",

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:05.010
just based on our videos they were like,

00:59:05.010 --> 00:59:08.138
you guys are musical, you're comedians,

00:59:08.138 --> 00:59:09.810
let's develop something together.

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:11.660
And we were like, well, we have an idea.

00:59:11.660 --> 00:59:14.500
Anybody contacts us from
Australia or New Zealand,

00:59:14.500 --> 00:59:16.070
we have an idea.

00:59:16.070 --> 00:59:20.400
Norman Normal, Norman is totally normal

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:23.020
except for one detail,
he owns a briefcase.

00:59:23.020 --> 00:59:26.070
And this is kind of a kid's animated show.

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:28.250
We're thinking more kids back then.

00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:30.130
Norman owns a brief briefcase

00:59:30.130 --> 00:59:34.540
that contains two pint sized
superheroes, Rhett and Link.

00:59:34.540 --> 00:59:35.760
- Hey guys, that's us.

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:38.140
- This might seem useful until
you learn that Rhett and Link

00:59:38.140 --> 00:59:39.810
each have a different superpower

00:59:39.810 --> 00:59:43.190
every single time Norman
opens the briefcase.

00:59:43.190 --> 00:59:46.110
- So Norman would be dealing
with the everyday problems-

00:59:46.110 --> 00:59:48.530
- He was a landlord of
an apartment building.

00:59:48.530 --> 00:59:51.180
- And so he had like
normal, not sensational,

00:59:51.180 --> 00:59:53.750
but just normal problems.

00:59:53.750 --> 00:59:56.495
You know, like, oh, there's a flooded pipe

00:59:56.495 --> 00:59:57.780
in this apartment complex.

00:59:57.780 --> 01:00:00.180
And so he would open up the briefcase

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:02.860
and Rhett would have one superpower

01:00:02.860 --> 01:00:04.370
and Link would have another superpower

01:00:04.370 --> 01:00:05.490
but it might be something
stupid like well,

01:00:05.490 --> 01:00:08.680
Rhett can shoot spaghetti
out of his butt hole

01:00:08.680 --> 01:00:10.130
and Link is invisible.

01:00:10.130 --> 01:00:12.100
So it's like, okay, what
can I do with spaghetti

01:00:12.100 --> 01:00:13.170
and somebody I can't see?

01:00:13.170 --> 01:00:15.220
And then he would close
it and open it immediately

01:00:15.220 --> 01:00:17.560
and it would be two completely
new novel superpowers.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:20.110
And he would do that until
he got two super powers

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:23.080
that would help him with
his mundane normal problem.

01:00:23.080 --> 01:00:26.660
- Right, and the cool thing was we,

01:00:26.660 --> 01:00:29.660
we pitched this and they were like,

01:00:29.660 --> 01:00:33.020
we like it, let's do something with it.

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:34.920
And they said, if you don't mind

01:00:34.920 --> 01:00:38.130
I could take a stab at
writing, starting the pilot.

01:00:38.130 --> 01:00:41.003
And remember, cause they
had written some stuff,

01:00:41.003 --> 01:00:43.660
they had produced some stuff.

01:00:43.660 --> 01:00:46.500
I think it was on like
Australian television.

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:48.886
- Yeah, I don't remember
many details about this.

01:00:48.886 --> 01:00:51.337
- So we kind of trusted 'em
and the dude wrote a pilot

01:00:51.337 --> 01:00:54.000
and it was, we were kind of blown away.

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:55.925
We were like, this is crazy.

01:00:55.925 --> 01:00:56.929
- Do you have a copy of that?

01:00:56.929 --> 01:00:58.688
- I'll look for it.

01:00:58.688 --> 01:00:59.760
- And why didn't anything
happen with that?

01:00:59.760 --> 01:01:02.590
- Because I think we got cold feet.

01:01:02.590 --> 01:01:04.270
Let's see if there's anything, nope,

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:06.870
don't have anything earlier
because I wasn't using,

01:01:06.870 --> 01:01:08.730
I didn't move it to my Google Drive.

01:01:08.730 --> 01:01:09.890
I could probably find it somewhere.

01:01:09.890 --> 01:01:11.270
- It might be on my Dropbox.

01:01:11.270 --> 01:01:13.940
- I mean, we've got some unscripted ideas.

01:01:13.940 --> 01:01:15.250
I don't want to go through those

01:01:15.250 --> 01:01:17.170
because they're more recent,

01:01:17.170 --> 01:01:18.960
they're based off of
like spinning off ideas

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:22.460
from "Good Mythical Morning"
into their own shows

01:01:22.460 --> 01:01:24.013
that we would host.

01:01:25.640 --> 01:01:27.600
That's when "Guesstimaters" came back

01:01:27.600 --> 01:01:30.347
as a show we were calling
"The Magic Number."

01:01:31.460 --> 01:01:35.430
- Norman Normal draft 1.6 doc.

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:36.540
- Oh, you found it.

01:01:36.540 --> 01:01:39.290
- Pilot episode beat sheet draft, 2009.

01:01:39.290 --> 01:01:43.780
- 2009, that's crazy.

01:01:43.780 --> 01:01:46.380
- Okay, opening title montage.

01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:50.400
Montage would be shot as
an action hero show open

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:53.090
from the 70s but slowly anti-climactic,

01:01:53.090 --> 01:01:56.040
showing anti-climactic
events, that's great.

01:01:56.040 --> 01:01:58.300
Norman is doing very
mundane everyday activities

01:01:58.300 --> 01:01:59.400
from brushing his teeth

01:01:59.400 --> 01:02:01.600
to minor repairs around
the apartment block.

01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:04.180
Rhett and Link are
singing the opening theme.

01:02:04.180 --> 01:02:06.260
Each of Norman's mundane tasks

01:02:06.260 --> 01:02:07.990
ends with a superpower moment,

01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:11.080
mostly bringing a smile to the situation

01:02:11.080 --> 01:02:12.690
and a quick cut to Rhett
and Link performing

01:02:12.690 --> 01:02:14.973
to acknowledge the origin
of the superpowers.

01:02:18.540 --> 01:02:23.273
Let's see if I, oh, yeah,
written by Andrew Horn.

01:02:26.410 --> 01:02:27.243
- Shout out to Andrew,

01:02:27.243 --> 01:02:29.850
thanks for believing
in us back in the day.

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:31.520
I mean, there's other
stuff we can unearth,

01:02:31.520 --> 01:02:33.220
maybe we'll do that at another time,

01:02:33.220 --> 01:02:37.340
but like that's pretty much
everything that I had unearthed

01:02:37.340 --> 01:02:39.040
that I wanted to talk about.

01:02:39.040 --> 01:02:41.660
I mean, I definitely remember
going in for the pitches,

01:02:41.660 --> 01:02:43.370
and I do think, like you said,

01:02:43.370 --> 01:02:47.396
we can talk more about like
what's the psychological impact

01:02:47.396 --> 01:02:52.396
on people who have all
these creative aspirations

01:02:52.650 --> 01:02:56.120
like in being rejected so many times?

01:02:56.120 --> 01:02:58.590
I mean, and sometimes
things get really far,

01:02:58.590 --> 01:03:01.570
like with "Legitimate",
we were sitting in a room

01:03:01.570 --> 01:03:05.033
at ABC Network-
- singing a song for them.

01:03:05.033 --> 01:03:09.251
- Singing this song and they
like all clapped afterwards.

01:03:09.251 --> 01:03:12.037
We were like, that was amazing.

01:03:12.037 --> 01:03:14.010
We nailed this song, we were talking about

01:03:14.010 --> 01:03:15.890
how Jack Black was going to die

01:03:15.890 --> 01:03:19.923
and they were eating
it up and then nothing.

01:03:22.178 --> 01:03:25.250
So I propose that, that we pick up,

01:03:25.250 --> 01:03:29.660
we talk more about the
existential crisis of rejection

01:03:31.150 --> 01:03:32.450
in the next episode.

01:03:32.450 --> 01:03:34.730
- So we'll take the next
episode to talk about what

01:03:34.730 --> 01:03:37.780
experiencing that level and
that amount of rejection

01:03:37.780 --> 01:03:41.110
has done and how that might relate to you

01:03:41.110 --> 01:03:42.150
and the things that you're trying to do.

01:03:42.150 --> 01:03:46.103
I will say that we live in a weird,

01:03:48.880 --> 01:03:51.760
we kind of have a unique
situation because,

01:03:51.760 --> 01:03:54.532
again, everything that
we just talked to you

01:03:54.532 --> 01:03:56.420
about is something that
a very small, until now,

01:03:56.420 --> 01:03:58.410
a very small group of people knew about

01:03:58.410 --> 01:04:00.920
and they all had mostly said, no,

01:04:00.920 --> 01:04:03.770
we're not interested in
this, but at the same time

01:04:05.090 --> 01:04:08.530
we've been able to have enough things work

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:11.210
to be able to make a living
in the entertainment industry

01:04:11.210 --> 01:04:13.670
in a very sort of non traditional

01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:15.680
sort of figure it out as you go along way

01:04:15.680 --> 01:04:19.070
through the digital media and in YouTube.

01:04:19.070 --> 01:04:22.660
And so it creates this lots of success

01:04:22.660 --> 01:04:25.351
that's sort of fueling
the growth of our company

01:04:25.351 --> 01:04:27.800
and being able to have the margin

01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:30.040
to develop these traditional ideas

01:04:30.040 --> 01:04:34.100
and to get like what you know,
what you might call rewarded

01:04:34.100 --> 01:04:37.540
and to be sort of accepted
by this group of people

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:39.670
who have decided that they
liked the things that we create

01:04:39.670 --> 01:04:41.420
that have made it into the world.

01:04:41.420 --> 01:04:44.900
But at the same time to
have this very long string

01:04:44.900 --> 01:04:47.930
of these things that we're
super passionate about

01:04:47.930 --> 01:04:49.140
that we're very excited about,

01:04:49.140 --> 01:04:50.090
that we're always thinking about

01:04:50.090 --> 01:04:51.440
and always trying to develop,

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:54.810
repeatedly told no about those things.

01:04:54.810 --> 01:04:57.480
There's a, I mean, again,
what I'm saying is that

01:04:57.480 --> 01:05:00.250
some people are just getting
told no all the time.

01:05:00.250 --> 01:05:02.570
You're a classic like starving artists who

01:05:02.570 --> 01:05:05.780
hasn't had anything work
and is just being told no

01:05:05.780 --> 01:05:08.453
and they may be super talented
and may have great ideas.

01:05:08.453 --> 01:05:11.790
We have the privilege of having
had success in this one area

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:14.991
that gives us the margin to be
able to develop these things

01:05:14.991 --> 01:05:17.670
and then actually have
somebody listen to them

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:19.150
and then be told no.

01:05:19.150 --> 01:05:21.910
But it is an interesting thing
that like what has it done

01:05:21.910 --> 01:05:24.040
to be told no that many times

01:05:24.040 --> 01:05:26.634
and why do we keep going right
back to the drawing board

01:05:26.634 --> 01:05:29.820
and believing that we are going
to be able to make something

01:05:29.820 --> 01:05:33.070
like one of these things
that we talked about.

01:05:33.070 --> 01:05:35.011
We'll talk about that next week.

