- For now, now #EarBiscuits if any,

01:05:37.650 --> 01:05:40.460
I mean, if you love any of
those and you want to buy 'em,

01:05:40.460 --> 01:05:43.330
if you're a network
executive, Hey, #EarBiscuits.

01:05:43.330 --> 01:05:44.890
- And I've got a quick rec

01:05:44.890 --> 01:05:48.570
that ties directly into this conversation

01:05:48.570 --> 01:05:50.590
and especially begins to bleed over

01:05:50.590 --> 01:05:53.040
into the conversation
that we'll have next week,

01:05:53.040 --> 01:05:54.905
which is something I've
been thinking about a lot.

01:05:54.905 --> 01:05:58.380
It's a book that's a very quick read,

01:05:58.380 --> 01:06:03.110
very easy read called "Big
Magic" by Elizabeth Gilbert.

01:06:03.110 --> 01:06:05.947
Now Elizabeth Gilbert is
the novelist who wrote

01:06:05.947 --> 01:06:07.670
"Eat, Pray, Love", which I have to say,

01:06:07.670 --> 01:06:10.800
I have not read the
book or seen the movie.

01:06:10.800 --> 01:06:11.633
- Me neither.

01:06:13.970 --> 01:06:16.530
- But my wife was listening
to or reading this book

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:19.040
and then she was like, I think
he might enjoy this book.

01:06:19.040 --> 01:06:21.240
It's like, it's all about,
it's a nonfiction book

01:06:21.240 --> 01:06:23.860
and it's all about the creative process

01:06:23.860 --> 01:06:25.910
and sort of the approach to creativity.

01:06:25.910 --> 01:06:28.112
I mean, one of the most
helpful books that I ever read

01:06:28.112 --> 01:06:30.850
in terms of creativity was
the "Artists Way", right,

01:06:30.850 --> 01:06:32.900
which I recommend to any creative people.

01:06:34.460 --> 01:06:36.103
And I think that big magic is,

01:06:38.520 --> 01:06:42.120
you know, it's similar in
that it is super inspirational

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:45.020
and encouraging to you
as a creative person.

01:06:45.020 --> 01:06:46.370
And I think one of the
points that she makes

01:06:46.370 --> 01:06:47.630
is that everyone is creative,

01:06:47.630 --> 01:06:49.510
you don't have to be a
professional creative person

01:06:49.510 --> 01:06:50.710
to be creative

01:06:50.710 --> 01:06:52.830
and you don't have to be a
professional creative person

01:06:52.830 --> 01:06:57.830
to create things and to live
a creative life, as she says.

01:06:57.960 --> 01:07:02.395
Anyway, there's a few things
in there that you were like,

01:07:02.395 --> 01:07:05.850
okay, I don't know if I believe fully

01:07:05.850 --> 01:07:07.690
all the things that you're saying

01:07:07.690 --> 01:07:09.770
in some of the things that, you know,

01:07:09.770 --> 01:07:12.840
there's sort of a super spiritual
side to the whole thing.

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.650
But I think regardless of
where you kind of come down

01:07:14.650 --> 01:07:17.430
on that particular aspect of it,

01:07:17.430 --> 01:07:20.130
it's one of the most encouraging books

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:22.423
and it's interesting that, you know,

01:07:22.423 --> 01:07:25.040
we just went through all these projects

01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:26.770
and we're talking about
failure in the next episode

01:07:26.770 --> 01:07:29.930
because she tells so many stories

01:07:29.930 --> 01:07:34.930
about how to get through that rejection

01:07:37.230 --> 01:07:38.510
and people not believing in you

01:07:38.510 --> 01:07:41.900
and how so easily you can let
those external expectations

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:44.370
that you tie to your work bring you down

01:07:44.370 --> 01:07:47.550
and keep you from getting
through to the next idea

01:07:47.550 --> 01:07:50.330
and just the nature of ideas,
anyway, highly recommend it

01:07:50.330 --> 01:07:52.100
for anybody who even considers themselves

01:07:52.100 --> 01:07:56.267
even possibly a little
bit creative, "Big Magic".

